WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award-,

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.370
Emmy- and Tony-nominated actor

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.239
you know from films
such as "Marriage Story,"

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.007
"House of Gucci"
and the "Star Wars" franchise.

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:09.676
He can be seen next
in "Ferrari,"

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:11.244
which will be in theaters
on Christmas Day.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:12.578
Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:13.946
our very good friend,
Adam Driver.

00:00:13.946 --> 00:00:16.649
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:32.532 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:34.000
Welcome back.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.201
-Thank you.
Thanks for having me.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.036
-I -- You know,
I was first introduced

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:38.671
to you on the show "Girls,"

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.206
and you were so funny
in that show.

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:42.775
And now you're in
so many wonderful dramas.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:46.012
It is such a joy for me
to see you become

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:48.514
one of our most accomplished
recent "SNL" hosts.

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:49.915
It is just a joy.
-Thank you very m-- [ Laughs ]

00:00:49.915 --> 00:00:52.551
-I feel like we get to see
that side of you.

00:00:52.551 --> 00:00:55.488
And you -- you just hosted
for the fourth time.

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.256
Have you learned things
between each time?

00:00:57.256 --> 00:00:59.625
Do you feel more comfortable?
-Yeah, I feel more comfortable.

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:01.293
You got to learn
when to freak out.

00:01:01.293 --> 00:01:04.997
Which the first two days,
you know, it's kind of a wash.

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:07.867
You know, they're very ritual,
and, you know.

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.069
But then -- then by Wednesday
you know to kind of get nervous.

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:11.504
-Yeah. That's good.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:14.573
-You do want to save
your sort of reserves of nerves.

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:16.042
If you waste them
the first two days,

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:20.146
then you, like, have a very
unearned Zen state on Saturday,

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:21.647
which is bad.
-But it does

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:25.117
just kind of go whether or not
you try to stop it or not.

00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:26.519
It's going to happen...
-Yeah.

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:27.854
-...and you can't control it,

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:29.855
so you just kind of have to roll
with it, yeah.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.258
-You -- I know from having
worked there,

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:34.494
nothing is better
than when a host comes

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:36.963
and shares one extra skill.

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:39.265
I loved your monologue this time
when you played the piano.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.367
-Thank you. Thanks.
-And, uh, were they --

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.536
were they -- I would imagine
they were very receptive

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:44.871
and happy
when you said you wanted

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:46.405
to do that for the monologue.

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:49.476
-Uh, yeah. Yeah, they --
they liked the idea.

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:50.809
The two writers
that they hooked --

00:01:50.809 --> 00:01:51.944
I can't remember
their names now,

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:53.112
but they were great.

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:55.347
And, uh...
-By the way, I saw them.

00:01:55.347 --> 00:01:57.016
-Terrible.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.985
-I saw them just
before the show,

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:00.220
and I said you were on,

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.654
and they said they didn't
remember you either.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.357
-[ Laughs ] Right.
But I remember Wally.

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.892
-Yeah. Oh, everybody
remembers Wally, huh?

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:09.462
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Um, it was really great.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:12.332
The movie as well,
"Ferrari" is wonderful.

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.000
And the last time you were here,
we were talking about --

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.335
I think you were maybe
just about to shoot

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:17.537
or just finished --
the great Michael Mann...

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:18.804
-Yeah.
-...is the director.

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:20.873
Did you have a favorite
Michael Mann film

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:22.675
before you started working
on this?

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:25.879
-Oh. Uh...
hard to -- Hard to say.

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:29.782
Uh, "Heat" and "The Insider"
are probably my two favorites.

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.818
And it kind of varies,
you know, month to month --

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.119
"Insider," "Heat."

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:35.955
But probably "Heat" has been
the most influential.

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.724
-Is he someone who likes
to talk about his old movies

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:40.826
when he's working
when you have downtime?

00:02:40.826 --> 00:02:42.795
-He doesn't have
a problem with it.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:46.132
He has a --
He's very researched,

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:49.669
so he has a lot of details
that that he's -- he's --

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.171
But mostly on set,
it's hard to pull him away

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:53.339
from what he's doing.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:56.141
He's very, you know,
myopically focused.

00:02:56.141 --> 00:02:58.110
-That's -- I mean, I guess
you kind of want your director

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.279
to be focused and, like, less,

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:00.680
"You want to hear
old showbiz stories."

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.081
-[ Laughs ] Right.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:04.984
-This -- Obviously there's
a great deal of driving

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:07.020
in this film, being that
it's the Ferrari film,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.288
but is it true
that you've been in a car

00:03:09.288 --> 00:03:10.890
with Michael Mann
while he's been driving?

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.258
-Oh, actually, that is true.
Yeah.

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:14.427
No one's asked me about that.
That was the most scar--

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:15.728
He has a Ferrari.
-Okay.

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:18.064
-And I've driven his Ferrari,

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:19.866
and that's the most scared
I've ever been.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:20.766
-Just sitting in
the passenger seat?

00:03:20.766 --> 00:03:22.201
-Sitting in the passenger seat.

00:03:22.201 --> 00:03:23.936
We drove to dinner, and he's
like, "Do you want to drive?"

00:03:23.936 --> 00:03:24.904
I'm like, "Yeah, I'll drive."

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.472
And then this is a mistake,

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:28.173
'cause he'll try
to direct you how --

00:03:28.173 --> 00:03:31.010
driving in California,
the roads are very, um,

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.478
they're like this.

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.647
So you don't want to scrape
the front part of --

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:37.116
-Oh, yeah, 'cause it's like --
So I wouldn't --

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:38.884
I mean, by the way,
I don't think I'm a good driver,

00:03:38.884 --> 00:03:40.386
but if someone was like,
"you want to drive my Ferrari,"

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:41.554
I'd be like, "This is terrible.
No."

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.189
-Yeah, yeah.
Especially with Michael.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.791
He's very, you know --
He's paying attention

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:46.326
to everything you're doing.

00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:47.826
-Is he the kind of person
that, like --

00:03:47.826 --> 00:03:49.895
Is he a talker in the car?

00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:51.297
-No, no, he's just focused on

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:54.934
how you're driving his car...
badly.

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.269
-That sounds like a disaster.

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.369
-It was not a good idea.
-Yeah.

00:03:57.369 --> 00:03:58.671
-Yeah. It was awful.

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:00.372
And we had raced up
until that point.

00:04:00.372 --> 00:04:02.141
Like, cars going fast.
-Yeah.

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:05.845
-These Radicals
and these Mazda Miatas,

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:07.880
which are very --
already a tight for me...

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.349
-Yeah.
-...to be in.

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:12.217
Even the Mazda Miata, my foot
went numb at a certain point

00:04:12.217 --> 00:04:13.286
because it's so small.

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:14.987
So I couldn't feel the shift,
you know?

00:04:14.987 --> 00:04:16.956
So I'm like,
"I got to get off the track."

00:04:16.956 --> 00:04:19.825
But that didn't bother me as
much as riding in a passenger --

00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:21.960
riding with Michael in the
passenger seat of his Ferrari.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.230
-I wonder,
like, at the Miata dealership,

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.798
when someone as tall
as you walks in,

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:27.266
if they're like,
"Uh, should we tell him?"

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:30.103
-[ Laughs ] Yeah. "No."
-Like, "This isn't for you."

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:33.005
Like, "Don't take it personally,
but you should go elsewhere."

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:34.807
-Yeah.
-Um, so in the film,

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:38.044
you play Enzo Ferrari,
and so you do not --

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:40.679
I know there are a few scenes
where we see you

00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:43.248
as a younger Enzo
behind the wheel of a car,

00:04:43.248 --> 00:04:45.484
but you didn't actually do
any driving in the film.

00:04:45.484 --> 00:04:48.788
-No. No, no, no, no. No.
That's the end of that answer.

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.923
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. Was there a reason?

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.326
Was it just that it took place
in a time in Enzo's life

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:54.660
where he wasn't doing
much driving?

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.529
-Yeah, well, yeah,
because he wasn't racing it,

00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:58.431
and the little,
uh, thing that we did race,

00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:00.032
it was like
a very historic vehicle

00:05:00.032 --> 00:05:01.400
so they don't want you
driving that around.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.269
-[ Chuckles ] Right.
-And also just insurance reasons

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:04.703
they don't want you driving
around these like --

00:05:04.703 --> 00:05:09.141
these kind of replica 801s
that are very dangerous.

00:05:09.141 --> 00:05:11.343
We raced those cars
in California,

00:05:11.343 --> 00:05:12.745
the ones I was talking about,

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:14.714
and then we raced some
contemporary Ferraris in Modena

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:17.049
and it becomes painfully --
And then I drove a chassis

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:18.784
of one of the cars
that they built

00:05:18.784 --> 00:05:21.220
that was a model of the cars
that we used in the vehicle,

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.290
and it becomes obvious right
away how dangerous they are.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.126
There's no roll cages,
there's no seat belts.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:29.295
You know, the goal was to be
thrown from the car

00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:30.696
as opposed to get stuck
in the car

00:05:30.696 --> 00:05:32.398
and burned to death, so...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:34.767
-Oh, so if you got thrown
from a car, it was working.

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:36.401
-It was a win, yeah.
Yeah, yeah.

00:05:36.401 --> 00:05:37.570
-As you're going
through the air...

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:38.770
-"This feels right."
-..."this feels perfect."

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:40.105
-Yeah, yeah. [ Chuckles ]

00:05:40.105 --> 00:05:42.174
-"I never thought this would
work, and here it is!"

00:05:42.174 --> 00:05:44.077
I have a lot more questions
to ask about "Ferrari."

00:05:44.077 --> 00:05:45.578
We'll be right back
with more Adam.

